matters and what's important of life and all

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:25.589
that other stuff.

00:10:25.589 --> 00:10:28.910
But don't also make them feel guilty for achieving
a lot of success.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:32.579
If that's important to them, let them do that
as well for them to go out there and live

00:10:32.579 --> 00:10:33.579
their dreams.

00:10:33.579 --> 00:10:37.990
So this is my suggestion to you as parents
and teenage entrepreneurs at this point, if

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:40.639
you haven't yet shared this with your parents,
do so.

00:10:40.639 --> 00:10:43.100
But I also have one last thing I want to challenge
parents.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.290
Listen, parents.

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:45.290
I want to speak to you.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:47.990
I grew up in a family, Middle-Eastern, mom
and dad.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:50.639
My mother's Armenian and my dad is Assyrian.

00:10:50.639 --> 00:10:53.339
My mother's last name is Boghosian and my
last name is Bet-David.

00:10:53.339 --> 00:10:54.360
My dad's last name is Bet-David.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:11:00.449
So, in a Middle-Eastern community, you're
expected to become three things when you grow

00:11:00.449 --> 00:11:03.830
up: lawyer, engineer, or doctor.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:07.360
That's just what's considered to be a kid
who became successful.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.100
So when all the parents talk among themselves,
they all say, "My son is a lawyer."

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.339
My son is a dentist.

00:11:14.339 --> 00:11:16.439
My son is an engineer for Raytheon.

00:11:16.439 --> 00:11:17.920
Oh my gosh!

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.379
And [with head hung low], Oh, my son works
at Sears.

00:11:20.379 --> 00:11:21.880
Oh, my son's in insurance.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:23.110
"Oh, he's in insurance.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.110
Oh, okay.

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:25.110
It's okay.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.110
It's going to be okay.

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:27.110
God is good."

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:28.470
all this type of stuff.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:34.589
Man, I am so glad I didn't become the engineer,
the lawyer or the doctor.

00:11:34.589 --> 00:11:36.110
I'm so glad I didn't take that route.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.930
I'm so glad that that didn't happen to me.

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:44.980
Not saying we don't need more engineers, doctors
or dentists or attorneys.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:45.980
I'm not saying that.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:50.660
All I'm telling you, I'm glad finally, I realized
I wanted to become an entrepreneur.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.199
But some of you guys struggle with one thing,
parents.

00:11:53.199 --> 00:11:56.480
And I feel bad for you because there's a lot
of pressure on parents today, and it really

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:57.480
bothers me.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:01.769
I go to a lot of different places as I speak
and parents always come up to me and they

00:12:01.769 --> 00:12:04.980
bring up this topic, especially recently.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.699
"Pat, what do I do?

00:12:06.699 --> 00:12:08.170
What school do I send my kids to?

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:12.230
My husband and I spoke, and we're willing
to sacrifice our 401K and take the money out.

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:14.870
The other day I talked to my tax and accountant
guy.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:16.810
We have $300,000 in our 401K.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.970
But if we take it out, we have to pay this
and 59 1/2.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.110
And what about this part?

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:25.339
We're willing to do all that stuff, just for
our kid to go out there and get a degree.

00:12:25.339 --> 00:12:26.339
Really?

00:12:26.339 --> 00:12:28.199
You really think that's the best thing for
you to do for your kid?

00:12:28.199 --> 00:12:30.069
You really think that's the best thing to
do for your kid?

00:12:30.069 --> 00:12:34.439
See, I think that's being questioned today
more than ever before because you got to realize.

00:12:34.439 --> 00:12:35.749
Do you know who started colleges?

00:12:35.749 --> 00:12:39.560
An entrepreneur started colleges and universities.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:42.780
So colleges and universities, the idea of
education was by somebody that said, what

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.920
if we can create a way for all of us to learn?

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:50.699
What if we can teach the future about school
and math and English and whatever their language

00:12:50.699 --> 00:12:51.699
was.

00:12:51.699 --> 00:12:53.980
That's how it started, many, many years ago,
right?

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:56.980
But it went from education into business.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:13:01.269
Some of the biggest businesses today in America
are non-profits called colleges and universities

00:13:01.269 --> 00:13:04.259
with $30 billion in their endowment contract.

00:13:04.259 --> 00:13:06.060
And they don't have to pay taxes for it.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:07.300
You and I do.

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:08.910
They don't have to pay taxes for it.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:13.881
They have more money than many of the Fortune
500 companies in their bank account, because

00:13:13.881 --> 00:13:16.480
you give them money and they pay this much
[zero] in taxes.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:18.389
Think about that for a moment right there.

00:13:18.389 --> 00:13:22.899
And I think we're at a moment right now that
maybe parents, a lot of them, are starting

00:13:22.899 --> 00:13:24.010
to question these things.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:27.480
Because parents, young parents nowadays, they're
50 years old, they're sitting there say, "Do

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:28.700
I really pay everything for this kid?"

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:33.480
So I wrote a book that many of your kids in
school are reading right now, many people

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.490
around the country are reading right now.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.839
And many parents are sharing this book with
each other.

00:13:38.839 --> 00:13:41.430
It's called Drop Out and Get Schooled.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:42.880
Drop Out and Get Schooled.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:45.999
In this book, we answer six questions:

00:13:45.999 --> 00:13:47.880
Why do we go to college in the first place?

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:49.870
Who should go to college and who shouldn't?

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:52.270
Can I succeed and do great things without
college?

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:55.430
Why do tuition and textbooks cost as much
as they do?

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:59.160
Have colleges simply become a big tax-free
business?

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:03.720
Are the subjects taught in college sufficient
for life or do we need an upgrade?

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.720
And there's a part dedicated purely to parents.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:08.060
I want you to read this.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.189
This is purely to parents.

00:14:09.189 --> 00:14:10.399
I want to hear your views.

00:14:10.399 --> 00:14:13.860
If you want to go read the reviews on Amazon,
the books only been out for a few weeks.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:15.180
Go read the reviews on Amazon.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.249
See what other people are saying about this
book.

00:14:17.249 --> 00:14:21.250
It's been already sold to over 30 countries
around the world and people are starting to

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:23.279
debate this topic and saying, this makes sense.

00:14:23.279 --> 00:14:25.350
I'm getting parents commenting.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:30.019
I'm getting parents - the other day a kid
13 year old kid bought the book with the mother's

00:14:30.019 --> 00:14:31.019
credit card.

00:14:31.019 --> 00:14:33.149
The mom not knowing - she left Amazon logged
on.

00:14:33.149 --> 00:14:34.269
The kid's reading the book.

00:14:34.269 --> 00:14:35.379
The mom says, give me the book.

00:14:35.379 --> 00:14:36.379
I want to read it, too.

00:14:36.379 --> 00:14:38.490
The mom reads it and says, this makes sense.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.339
And she sends me an Instagram post.

00:14:40.339 --> 00:14:44.160
And says, "My kid read this" on an Instagram
post.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:46.759
I want you to read it and I want to hear from
you.

00:14:46.759 --> 00:14:48.779
By the way, you post your reviews on Amazon.

00:14:48.779 --> 00:14:49.970
I look at every single one of them.

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:52.800
If you post your reviews on Amazon, I read
every single one of the reviews on Amazon.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:57.540
But if you want to personally snap me as well,
my snap code is here.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.029
It should be here to the right.

00:14:59.029 --> 00:15:00.120
Send me a snap.

